<ContentLayout
  title="SEO Audit Consultant: Hire a Dedicated Specialist"
  description="An SEO audit consultant diagnoses organic search problems specifically — rankings, crawlability, content gaps. Hourly rates from $150/hr, project engagements from $897."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  schema={schema}
  showLeadForm={true}
  publishDate="2026-08-26"
>

<p>An SEO audit consultant is one specialist assigned to your account. That's different from a team splitting the work across technical, content, and security specialists. This single-point-of-contact model suits businesses with a specific ranking problem who want one expert who knows the whole picture — a broader <a href="/website-audit-services/consultant/">website audit consultant</a> covers security and accessibility alongside SEO instead.</p>

<h2 id="what-an-seo-audit-consultant-does">What an SEO Audit Consultant Does</h2>

<p>The consultant runs the crawl, reviews Search Console data, benchmarks your competitors, and writes the findings personally rather than delegating to a production team. You get direct access to ask follow-up questions during and after the engagement. The same person handles any re-check after you've implemented fixes.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-hire-one">When to Hire One</h2>

<p>Three situations call for a dedicated consultant over a packaged audit product. A traffic drop with no obvious cause in Search Console needs someone who can work through hypotheses interactively instead of running a fixed checklist. A pre-migration review benefits from a consultant available for questions throughout the migration rather than a one-time report. An ongoing retainer relationship works better with continuity — the same person tracking your site over months notices patterns a fresh auditor each time would miss.</p>

<h2 id="consultant-vs-agency-vs-software">Consultant vs Agency vs Software</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Option</th>
      <th>Best For</th>
      <th>Trade-off</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Dedicated consultant</td>
      <td>Ongoing relationship, complex or unusual problems</td>
      <td>Higher hourly cost than packaged audits</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Full audit service</td>
      <td>One-off diagnosis at a fixed price</td>
      <td>No ongoing relationship with the same analyst</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Software tools</td>
      <td>Teams with in-house SEO expertise</td>
      <td>You still need to interpret the output yourself</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="pricing">Pricing</h2>

<p>Hourly engagements run $150-$300 depending on scope and urgency. Project-based engagements start at $897 for a defined diagnosis-and-fix-list scope. Ongoing retainers are quoted after an initial project.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

<h3>How is an SEO audit consultant different from the SEO audit service?</h3>
<p>The <a href="/seo-audit-services/">SEO audit service</a> is a fixed-scope, fixed-price product delivered by whoever is available. A consultant is one named specialist working with you directly. Pricing runs hourly or by project and suits ongoing or unusual engagements rather than a one-off diagnosis.</p>

<h3>Do you offer a trial hour before committing to a project?</h3>
<p>Yes. A single paid hour lets you assess fit before committing to a larger project or retainer.</p>

<h3>Can the consultant also implement fixes?</h3>
<p>Some consultants offer implementation at an agreed hourly rate. Confirm this during scoping since not every engagement includes it by default.</p>

</ContentLayout>
